00:30:13,845 --> 00:30:15,278
-It does?
-Yeah.

472
00:30:15,713 --> 00:30:17,977
-Let's do it, then.
-Okay.

473
00:30:19,350 --> 00:30:21,318
Damn you, Old Spice.

474
00:30:24,789 --> 00:30:28,520
So you run this pizza place
and you have your poetry.

475
00:30:29,527 --> 00:30:31,427
What brings you to the big city?

476
00:30:31,496 --> 00:30:34,021
Actually, I just inherited $40 billion.

477
00:30:35,133 --> 00:30:39,331
Well, that's nice, but I think anything
over $30 billion is just gravy.

478
00:30:42,373 --> 00:30:43,601
I like that.

479
00:30:44,709 --> 00:30:47,610
Your hair is very blond and pretty.

480
00:30:47,679 --> 00:30:50,978
I'm of Swedish ancestry.

481
00:30:51,783 --> 00:30:53,307
-Really?
-Yes.

482
00:30:54,219 --> 00:30:57,154
My grandfather was in ABBA.

483
00:30:58,623 --> 00:31:00,250
ABBA, the band?

484
00:31:00,892 --> 00:31:04,726
Excuse me,
do you get any famous people in here?

485
00:31:04,796 --> 00:31:06,195
Famous, rich, powerful.

486
00:31:06,264 --> 00:31:09,131
Really? We don't get
any famous people in Mandrake Falls.

487
00:31:09,200 --> 00:31:11,691
Stephen King stopped by
our gas station one time...

488
00:31:11,769 --> 00:31:14,169
...but everyone was too scared to talk to him.

489
00:31:14,239 --> 00:31:16,036
I'd imagine he'd be very spooky.

490
00:31:16,107 --> 00:31:19,508
Jake, the gas station attendant,
made eye contact with him.

491
00:31:19,577 --> 00:31:21,545
That night he lost 200 pounds.

492
00:31:23,748 --> 00:31:24,737
Wow.

493
00:31:25,683 --> 00:31:27,878
Your chicken parmesan should be ready soon.

494
00:31:27,952 --> 00:31:32,184
Okay, buddy. I saw a couple over there.
It's their anniversary.

495
00:31:32,824 --> 00:31:35,622
Give them this. Don't say it's from us, though.

496
00:31:36,361 --> 00:31:37,828
This is like $20,000.

497
00:31:37,896 --> 00:31:41,332
Tell them it was a restaurant giveaway
or something. Thanks, buddy.

498
00:31:43,001 --> 00:31:46,437
That's an awfully nice way
to start spending your money.

499
00:31:46,504 --> 00:31:48,495
-Is that who I think it is?
-Who?

500
00:31:49,140 --> 00:31:52,007
Preston Blake's nephew, the $40-billion man.

501
00:31:52,911 --> 00:31:54,435
We have to meet him. Dave?

502
00:31:55,113 --> 00:31:58,105
Gosh, I hope that mugger got home okay.

503
00:31:58,449 --> 00:31:59,814
-Mr. Deeds.
-Yeah.

504
00:31:59,884 --> 00:32:02,910
Mr. Simonds would like
to invite you to join his party.

505
00:32:03,488 --> 00:32:04,477
Why is that?

506
00:32:05,056 --> 00:32:07,251
Just to enjoy the pleasure of your company.

507
00:32:07,325 --> 00:32:09,350
Wow, that's pretty cool of him.

508
00:32:13,731 --> 00:32:15,528
That was a very nice hug, Deeds.

509
00:32:15,967 --> 00:32:19,232
It's wicked nice of you to invite us over.
This is awesome.

510
00:32:19,537 --> 00:32:22,597
-Have we met?
-Not unless you have a sick kid.

511
00:32:23,041 --> 00:32:26,272
You're a very important man
in this town now, Mr. Deeds.

512
00:32:26,344 --> 00:32:27,777
I don't know about that.

513
00:32:27,845 --> 00:32:29,676
I sing at the Metropolitan Opera...

514
00:32:29,747 --> 00:32:33,444
...and Blake Media contributes
50 percent of our budget.

515
00:32:33,518 --> 00:32:35,452
So, on behalf of the opera:

516
00:32:35,520 --> 00:32:39,479
"Thank you"

517
00:32:42,627 --> 00:32:43,958
That was awesome!

518
00:32:44,395 --> 00:32:47,558
You shouldn't thank me, though.
You should thank my great-uncle.

519
00:32:47,632 --> 00:32:49,031
He was an interesting man.

520
00:32:49,100 --> 00:32:51,330
I served on the board
of the Guggenheim with him.

521
00:32:51,402 --> 00:32:54,337
Really? I'm not sure what that is,
but good luck with it.

522
00:32:55,873 --> 00:32:58,706
I'd love to write a piece on you
for The New Yorker.

523
00:32:58,776 --> 00:33:00,368
What's The New Yorker?

524
00:33:01,279 --> 00:33:02,405
It's a magazine.

525
00:33:03,414 --> 00:33:06,110
With essays, witty cartoons.

526
00:33:06,818 --> 00:33:10,777
I read the Mandrake Falls Gazette
and they got some cartoons in there also.

527
00:33:10,855 --> 00:33:13,415
You know who's a riot? That Beetle Bailey guy.

528
00:33:13,524 --> 00:33:16,118
He's always wearing his hat and doing his thing.

529
00:33:16,728 --> 00:33:19,526
But it is an honor to meet
a real writer like you, George.

530
00:33:19,597 --> 00:33:22,031
I've been trying
to get my stuff published for years...

531
00:33:22,100 --> 00:33:24,534
...but Hallmark is one tough cookie to crack.

532
00:33:24,602 --> 00:33:27,162
-Hallmark?
-He writes greeting cards.

533
00:33:27,872 --> 00:33:31,069
-I try.
-You must share one with us.

534
00:33:31,142 --> 00:33:34,737
-I don't know about that, Mr. French.
-Who's Mr. French?

535
00:33:34,812 --> 00:33:38,111
-Doesn't he look like Mr. French?
-I've never heard of him.

536
00:33:38,182 --> 00:33:39,149
Come on.

537
00:33:39,217 --> 00:33:40,844
All right, I'll give you one.

538
00:33:40,918 --> 00:33:43,546
This is a Mother's Day card I've been working on.

539
00:33:43,621 --> 00:33:47,717
"Mom, you are the one
who brought me to planet Earth

540
00:33:48,593 --> 00:33:51,721
"You are the one who suffered
through my 14-hour birth

541
00:33:52,430 --> 00:33:56,389
"You are the one who made lemonade for me
after I'd come back from play

542
00:33:56,801 --> 00:34:00,931
"I love you, Mom,
so have a wicked nice Mother's Day"

543
00:34:01,439 --> 00:34:02,838
Something like that.

544
00:34:03,641 --> 00:34:06,872
-An instant classic!
-"Genius, genius, genius"

545
00:34:07,245 --> 00:34:09,270
Do you write your poems with a crayon?

546
00:34:13,618 --> 00:34:14,880
It's amazing!

547
00:34:16,220 --> 00:34:18,984
I see why you brought me here.
To goof on me, huh?

548
00:34:19,590 --> 00:34:22,115
-Don't be silly, Deeds.
-Pipe down, George.

549
00:34:22,827 --> 00:34:25,694
I may seem funny to you,
but if you came to Mandrake Falls...

550
00:34:25,763 --> 00:34:28,823
...you might seem funny to us.
Only nobody would laugh at you...

551
00:34:28,900 --> 00:34:31,835
...and make you feel stupid,
that wouldn't be good manners.

552
00:34:31,903 --> 00:34:35,498
Maybe my poems aren't that great,
but I know some people who like them.

553
00:34:35,673 --> 00:34:39,700
Anyways, it's the best I can do.
We're going to get going.

554
00:34:39,777 --> 00:34:43,008
Sorry about that, Pam.
I hope I didn't embarrass you.

555
00:34:48,019 --> 00:34:51,648
And if it wasn't for Miss Dawson being here,
I'd knock your heads in.

556
00:34:51,722 --> 00:34:52,916
I don't mind.

557
00:34:53,224 --> 00:34:54,213
Okay.

558
00:34:57,895 --> 00:34:58,884
"No"

559
00:35:05,570 --> 00:35:09,472
I think I just shat myself.

560
00:35:10,341 --> 00:35:12,741
How would you like to see the sights now?

561
00:35:12,810 --> 00:35:15,335
I just need to powder my nose.

562
00:35:15,413 --> 00:35:17,813
That would be great. I'll get your coat.

563
00:35:18,449 --> 00:35:22,249
Hey, buddy, this will pay for everything
I damaged. Sorry about that.

564
00:35:22,320 --> 00:35:24,720
-Good to meet you guys.
-Thank you.

565
00:35:24,789 --> 00:35:26,416
God, that was good stuff.

566
00:35:29,994 --> 00:35:31,461
I want a raise, Mac.

567
00:35:35,900 --> 00:35:37,731
Hey, man! What's up?

568
00:35:38,035 --> 00:35:40,435
Holy cannoli, you're John McEnroe!

569
00:35:40,505 --> 00:35:43,565
I saw what you did to those guys
making fun of you. Nice work.

570
00:35:43,641 --> 00:35:46,610
You know what it's like to get riled up
don't you, Johnny Mac?

571
00:35:46,677 --> 00:35:49,510
-That I do.
-I'm Deeds, by the way.

572
00:35:49,580 --> 00:35:53,311
Deeds, nice to meet you.
You got to let me buy you a drink.

573
00:35:53,384 --> 00:35:55,181
Miss Dawson, this is John McEnroe.

574
00:35:55,253 --> 00:35:58,916
-Miss Dawson, nice to meet you.
-Nice to meet you, too.

575
00:35:59,123 --> 00:36:01,648
I promised Miss Dawson I'd take her sightseeing.

576
00:36:01,726 --> 00:36:04,991
The sights are great.
I've seen the sights. I love the sights.

577
00:36:05,062 --> 00:36:08,156
Let me show you a New York
most people will never see.

578
00:36:08,633 --> 00:36:12,034
-That okay with you, Miss Dawson?
-Sounds like fun!

579
00:36:12,103 --> 00:36:14,571
Here's to a big night on the town.

580
00:36:27,051 --> 00:36:28,712
What happened?

581
00:36:28,819 --> 00:36:33,051
Here are some aspirins, Mr. Deeds.
They make your head seem smaller.

582
00:36:37,061 --> 00:36:40,224
-How did I get in these pajamas?
-I changed you.

583
00:36:40,765 --> 00:36:42,460
I was very gentle, sir.

584
00:36:43,935 --> 00:36:47,268
I think I drank all the alcohol in Manhattan
last night.

585
00:36:47,572 --> 00:36:50,132
And you nearly succeeded,
according to Miss Dawson.

586
00:36:51,475 --> 00:36:55,343
Miss Dawson! I don't even remember
taking her home. Is she okay?

587
00:36:55,413 --> 00:36:58,473
I took the liberty of sending her off
in the company limousine.

588
00:36:58,549 --> 00:37:01,484
She was a lady in distress and I helped her.

589
00:37:02,086 --> 00:37:03,747
Dreamt about her last night.

590
00:37:04,288 --> 00:37:07,052
Usually, when you get blackout drunk
you don't dream.

591
00:37:08,626 --> 00:37:09,684
So I read.

592
00:37:14,298 --> 00:37:15,629
What's up, Chuck?

593
00:37:19,136 --> 00:37:21,297
America's newest billionaire...

594
00:37:23,007 --> 00:37:25,066
-...stupid and drunk.
-What's this?

595
00:37:25,142 --> 00:37:28,077
It was quite a night for Longfellow Deeds.

596
00:37:28,145 --> 00:37:30,739
They'll know my name is Longfellow.

597
00:37:31,215 --> 00:37:33,581
After punching out some society types...

598
00:37:35,119 --> 00:37:39,522
...Deeds joined forces with the original
bad boy of tennis, John McEnroe.

599
00:37:39,824 --> 00:37:42,292
And stupidity won in straight sets.

600
00:37:49,233 --> 00:37:50,962
How'd they get that footage?

601
00:37:51,469 --> 00:37:53,096
Watch out, Johnny!

602
00:37:55,806 --> 00:37:58,969
What kind of driving is that?
You're a fucking disgrace!

603
00:37:59,610 --> 00:38:01,601
No, sir. You're a disgrace.

604
00:38:01,679 --> 00:38:04,011
-We did it, man!
-Johnny McEnroe!

605
00:38:04,081 --> 00:38:07,414
Look at Deedsy hanging out with McEnroe!
That's awesome!

606
00:38:08,552 --> 00:38:10,042
I love the Beach Boys.

607
00:38:10,788 --> 00:38:12,915
Here I go, here I go.

608
00:38:15,660 --> 00:38:18,356
This is not the kind of attention
we need right now.

609
00:38:18,529 --> 00:38:19,621
What do you mean?

610
00:38:19,697 --> 00:38:22,564
For the moment you're the largest stockholder...

611
00:38:22,633 --> 00:38:25,193
...and people might think
any instability you show...

612
00:38:25,269 --> 00:38:27,430
...may equal instability in the company.

613
00:38:31,509 --> 00:38:32,498
My bad.

614
00:38:34,545 --> 00:38:36,809
My bad. My bad.

615
00:38:36,881 --> 00:38:37,870
Okay. Okay.

616
00:38:37,948 --> 00:38:38,937
-My bad.
-Okay.

617
00:38:39,083 --> 00:38:42,280
Shall I call your car, Mr. Cedar,
to take you to the funeral?

618
00:38:42,353 --> 00:38:43,684
What funeral?

619
00:38:44,588 --> 00:38:46,249
Your great-uncle's funeral.

620
00:38:46,324 --> 00:38:48,292
-His funeral's today?
-Today, yes.

621
00:38:48,659 --> 00:38:50,422
I'll go get my suit on.

622
00:38:57,935 --> 00:39:00,062
-Cute, bigmouth.
-Sir?

623
00:39:00,504 --> 00:39:02,802
As soon as that moron goes back
to Cowpie Falls...

624
00:39:02,873 --> 00:39:05,603
...you are out of here
on your fat Puerto Rican ass.

625
00:39:10,548 --> 00:39:12,277
I hail from Spain, sir.

626
00:39:18,456 --> 00:39:19,684
Ol?

627
00:39:21,092 --> 00:39:25,153
Now, Brother Preston is soaring
with eagles high above.

628
00:39:26,430 --> 00:39:29,024
Because he lived a life of love.

629
00:39:30,634 --> 00:39:33,467
Yes, he's flying way up high...

630
00:39:34,438 --> 00:39:36,998
...because he was a supercool guy.

631
00:39:38,342 --> 00:39:41,709
He's gone away, too soon, it seems...

632
00:39:42,346 --> 00:39:45,247
...leaving behind his unfinished dreams.

633
00:39:45,616 --> 00:39:48,346
This guy could make a fortune
writing greeting cards.

634
00:39:48,419 --> 00:39:51,252
Yes, we remember Preston Blake...

635
00:39:51,789 --> 00:39:54,622
...a man with faith no man could shake.

636
00:39:55,459 --> 00:39:57,620
A strength no man could break.

637
00:39:58,162 --> 00:40:00,653
A character no man could fake.

638
00:40:01,465 --> 00:40:04,025
For goodness sake, let's eat some cake.

639
00:40:04,402 --> 00:40:05,869
Amen.

640
00:40:07,772 --> 00:40:10,741
Excuse me, Reverend, that was a grand slam.

641
00:40:10,808 --> 00:40:13,743
That part with soaring with the eagles
gave me goosebumps.

642
00:40:13,811 --> 00:40:17,338
-Thank you, my brother.
-My friend got me a mug for my birthday.

643
00:40:17,415 --> 00:40:21,249
It says, "It's hard to soar with the eagles
when you're surrounded by turkeys."

644
00:40:21,318 --> 00:40:23,047
But what you said was better.

645
00:40:23,454 --> 00:40:24,751
It's all good.

646
00:40:25,156 --> 00:40:29,058
Rest in peace, Preston.
That concludes today's service.

647
00:40:30,594 --> 00:40:32,926
I actually thought I would say something.

648
00:40:34,698 --> 00:40:37,292
Deedsy was always the best speaker in school.

649
00:40:38,068 --> 00:40:38,932
Deedsy?

650
00:40:39,003 --> 00:40:41,164
That's some job, by the way, Your Majesty.

651
00:40:41,505 --> 00:40:43,496
I thought we were watching Scooby-Doo.

652
00:40:44,341 --> 00:40:48,505
Hi, my name is Deeds.
I was Preston Blake's great-nephew.

653
00:40:48,913 --> 00:40:52,246
I wrote a poem for him
on my way over here, in my head.

654
00:40:52,583 --> 00:40:55,017
And I'm going to recite it for you.

655
00:40:55,686 --> 00:40:59,679
I didn't realize I'd be following
the rhyme-master Reverend Sharpton.

656
00:41:00,124 --> 00:41:01,751
But here we go anyways.

657
00:41:02,359 --> 00:41:05,328
"You climbed mountains and built skyscrapers

658
00:41:06,530 --> 00:41:10,432
"You made TV shows and put out newspapers

659
00:41:12,102 --> 00:41:14,696
"You were wicked good at doing stocks

660
00:41:15,840 --> 00:41:18,308
"You liked it
when Emilio would change your socks

661
00:41:19,977 --> 00:41:22,775
"We never hung out and that makes me sad

662
00:41:23,647 --> 00:41:25,979
"AII the good times we could've had"

663
00:41:26,684 --> 00:41:28,117
Nice rhyme.

664
00:41:28,586 --> 00:41:32,044
"But when I die, Uncle Preston,
you'd better say, 'Cheers!'

665
00:41:32,823 --> 00:41:35,485
"'Cause me and you are hanging
at the pearly gates

666
00:41:35,559 --> 00:41:39,495
"I'll bring the beers, I'll bring the beers"

667
00:41:40,197 --> 00:41:41,255
He's good.

668
00:41:41,799 --> 00:41:42,823
Amen.

669
00:41:42,900 --> 00:41:46,563
I know this is supposed to be
a closed casket, but where I come from...

670
00:41:46,637 --> 00:41:50,300
...we feel it's good to see the body
for the grieving process, so....

671
00:41:53,277 --> 00:41:56,269
Sorry about that! I'll keep this sucker closed.

672
00:42:01,051 --> 00:42:02,882
He's still pretty frozen.

673
00:42:03,521 --> 00:42:04,749
Almost there.

674
00:42:07,024 --> 00:42:09,458
I'll keep an eye on the company for you.
Love you.

675
00:42:09,527 --> 00:42:11,722
Nice meeting you. Thanks for the apartment.

676
00:42:12,263 --> 00:42:13,457
That was freaky.

677
00:42:13,964 --> 00:42:15,522
Everybody drive home safe.

678
00:42:15,833 --> 00:42:17,926
Man, this guy is in way over his head.

679
00:42:18,002 --> 00:42:20,163
-Take care.
-You'll see him tonight?

680
00:42:20,237 --> 00:42:23,570
Yes, I'm calling around 4:00.
That's when I get off work.

681
00:42:23,807 --> 00:42:29,404
Remember, I'm Pam Dawson, virgin school nurse
from Winchestertonfieldville, lowa.

682
00:42:31,849 --> 00:42:33,043
That's priceless.

683
00:42:34,685 --> 00:42:35,777
You, a virgin.

684
00:42:42,459 --> 00:42:44,984
-All right, thank you.
-Excuse me, sir.

685
00:42:45,396 --> 00:42:47,830
-I told him he could have that.
-Thanks.

686
00:42:47,898 --> 00:42:49,490
Right on. Nice seeing you.

687
00:42:49,767 --> 00:42:52,565
You need to use our bathroom,
come back and use it, okay?

688
00:42:52,636 --> 00:42:54,604
-Will do!
-All right.

689
00:42:56,073 --> 00:42:58,234
How do you think I did at the funeral today?

690
00:42:58,309 --> 00:42:59,833
You spoke beautifully, sir.

691
00:43:00,277 --> 00:43:01,403
I still feel bad.

692
00:43:01,478 --> 00:43:05,175
I snapped my uncle's hand off
when I put him back in the casket.

693
00:43:05,649 --> 00:43:07,583
Lucky for him, he was dead, sir.

694
00:43:08,085 --> 00:43:10,519
Even though his face was frozen that way...

695
00:43:11,021 --> 00:43:13,854
...it looked to me like he was a happy guy.
Am I right?

696
00:43:14,358 --> 00:43:17,054
-He truly was.
-Come on, sit down, please.

697
00:43:17,561 --> 00:43:21,019
And I got to hang out with him, just like this...

698
00:43:21,532 --> 00:43:23,659
...almost every day for 30 years.

699
00:43:24,168 --> 00:43:25,897
What did you guys talk about?

700
00:43:25,970 --> 00:43:30,134
We spoke of life, love, art, wine, business.

701
00:43:30,541 --> 00:43:32,236
I learned much from him.

702
00:43:32,543 --> 00:43:34,477
-I bet you miss him, too.
-I do.

703
00:43:34,878 --> 00:43:36,812
He was like a parent to me.

704
00:43:37,548 --> 00:43:40,642
You see, I never knew my father, and Mama...

705
00:43:41,218 --> 00:43:43,709
...she passed while giving the birth to me.

706
00:43:46,957 --> 00:43:49,221
-You want some Cocoa Pebbles?
-No, thanks.

707
00:43:50,127 --> 00:43:53,426
I have much work to do.

708
00:43:53,731 --> 00:43:57,030
-Thanks. You don't have to do that.
-Enjoy your Pebbles.

709
00:43:58,402 --> 00:43:59,835
One more thing.

710
00:44:00,437 --> 00:44:03,873
Your great-uncle did not let me
change his socks for him, either.

711
00:44:05,009 --> 00:44:06,271
I like feet.

712
00:44:07,244 --> 00:44:08,438
I do not know why.

713
00:44:17,087 --> 00:44:17,951
Hello.

714
00:44:18,022 --> 00:44:20,684
Hi, Deeds? It's Pam. Pam Dawson.

715
00:44:20,924 --> 00:44:24,951
I'm so happy you called.
I'm wicked sorry about last night.

716
00:44:25,029 --> 00:44:26,428
